naval ravikant you probably know him as  the founder of angellist  a prolific twitter philosopher or one of  the few silicon valley insiders to ever  appear on the joe rogan experience  but his life story is actually full of  ups and downs that inform who he is  today  and can be really instructive to  prospective entrepreneurs  i'm john coogan and this is the saga of  nival ravikant  naval was born in new delhi india in  1974  but moved to new york city when he was  just nine years old  his parents separated when he was still  young so he was raised by his mother in  queens while she worked long hours and  attended night school  consequently neval had long stretches of  time where he was left to his own  devices  which he made very good use of here's  naval describing his upbringing  on the joe rogan experience i was raised  by my  i was raised by a single mom in new york  and she used the local library as a  daycare center because it was a very  tough neighborhood and so she would  basically say when you get back from  school go straight to the library and  don't come out  until i pick you up late at night so i  used to basically live in the library  and i read everything i read every  magazine i read every  pictograph i read every book or every  map i just ran out of stuff to read i  just read everything  his voracious appetite for knowledge  served him well and he was able to test  into a public magnet high school  named stuvescent by performing well on  the admissions test  since his mother didn't earn enough  money to pay for a private school  a tuition-free public school like  student was really the only option for  naval to gain a strong education  he took his studies seriously and was  able to attend dartmouth after  graduation  with the help of a financial aid package  naval didn't go directly into technology  or entrepreneurship after graduation  though  instead he went to work for the boston  consulting group where he got exposed to  the inner workings of large corporations  and learned some business fundamentals  management consulting is a common  jumping off point into other careers  since it gives you a good overview of a  variety of different businesses  and instills strong analytical skills so  neval  left consulting to go work at a  high-speed cable internet provider  named at home this was in the late 90s  when internet stocks were booming  and at home was no exception at home  merged with excite  a popular web portal at the time and the  combined company was worth seven billion  dollars  but nevall had higher aspirations than  growing rich in a comfortable position  at a late stage tech company  he wanted to start something of his own  so he walked away from four million  dollars worth of unvested at-home stock  options  and joined august capital as an  entrepreneur in residence to start  planning his venture  this turned out to be a great move since  the high flying company did not make it  through the dot-com crash in 2001  at the peak of the bubble the company  was worth 35 billion dollars  but by october of 2001 the company had  filed for chapter 11 bankruptcy  protection  and naval shares would have been  worthless if he had stuck around  naval quickly assembled a team of  co-founders and raised a massive seed  round of 8 million dollars for this new  company  half of the funding came from august  capital and the other half came from  benchmark capital  and this funding round was believed to  be one of the highest seed valuations  for a company  ever starting a technology company was  much more capital intensive in the 90s  you had to buy actual server hardware  since aws  didn't exist yet so neval's close ties  to venture capital  really helped him get started here but  it wouldn't all be smooth sailing  his new company was called e-pinions and  the idea was simple  create a place on the internet where  anyone could review anything  the site had sections for reviews of  cars computers  and even physical places like ski  resorts this was all before yelp and  there weren't many competitors in the  space  neval served as ceo and grew the company  incredibly quickly  hiring 31 people in the first 12 weeks  less than one year after launching the  site epinions was able to close a 25  million dollar series a  but things started to get difficult for  the company after the dot-com bubble  popped later that year  the site reached nearly 6 million users  in 2003  but valuations of tech companies had  fallen so much  that there wasn't much hope for an ipo  noval and three of his co-founders left  the company to move on to new projects  leaving narav talia and the vcs from  benchmark in august  in charge of the company when growth  stagnates it's not uncommon to see a  change in leadership at a startup  and with 45 million dollars invested in  e-pinions the venture capitalists were  probably just focused on getting the  company to acquisition  so they didn't lose their entire  investment while naval had moved back  into the venture capital world  he received some news about e-pinions  the company would be merged with a  comparison pricing site named deal time  but the common stock that naval owned  would be worth nothing  naval and his co-founders approved the  merger which is completely reasonable  if the company you founded is struggling  and a merger will keep the product alive  and allow the company to avoid layoffs  it's immoral to block that merger on  principle  you gain nothing and it just winds up  hurting employees customers and  investors  but after the merger was completed  things got much more complicated  e-pinions and deal time relaunched at  shopping.com  and in 2004 the company went public at a  valuation of 750 million  everyone made a killing in the ipo but  naval and his co-founders had nothing to  show for it  so they sued in the lawsuit naval and  his fellow plaintiffs  alleged that their co-founder nirav  talia and the vc firms  had misrepresented the value of  e-pinions at the time of the merger  in order to get them to give up their  shares lawsuits between founders and  venture capitalists are extremely rare  this is because vc firms have vastly  greater resources to  fight off lawsuits and no founder wants  to be known  as someone who sues their investors it's  usually better just to move on  bury the hatchet and start something new  with a different group of investors  but naval had good reason to believe he  was being misled  especially after documents showed that  e-pinions was on the verge of signing a  deal with google  that would more than 10x the company's  profit right after the merger  they wound up settling the lawsuit out  of court so we don't know exactly what  happened  but i think a few things are likely here  the first  is that naval definitely got paid it's  unclear how big the settlement was  but naval was able to raise a 20 million  early stage venture capital fund  shortly after the settlement so it's  likely he got at least a few million  when you're raising a vc fund it's  typically expected that 10 to 20  of the fund will come from your own  capital there are of course exceptions  but it's unlikely that he would have  been able to close this fund without a  few million in personal capital  it's also extremely likely that everyone  signed a mutual non-disclosure agreement  and non-disparagement agreement as part  of the settlement  meaning that naval could never talk  publicly about exactly what had  happened even though naval couldn't  discuss the opinion settlement  stories about a young founder  successfully suing his vcs  echoed around silicon valley and helped  him earn a name  as someone who had been through the  wringer once again  naval was able to capitalize on the  opportunity in front of him incredibly  well  he started writing a blog called venture  hacks which offered detailed advice on  negotiating term sheets  and other information usually reserved  for experienced  silicon valley insiders he also started  angel list  with the goal of democratizing the  startup investment process  naval has spent the last decade laser  focused on the early stage fundraising  process  and he's grown angel lists significantly  adding products around recruiting  cryptocurrency and equity crowdfunding  and he's also continued to carve out  innovative content marketing strategies  and created a very unique voice on  twitter in one of his most successful  threads  titled how to get rich without getting  lucky he  maps out his entire philosophy on wealth  accumulation  in a series of extremely succinct tweets  the thesis of the thread  is that there are no get rich quick  schemes but by focusing on developing  specific knowledge  that can't be easily taught in schools  and then applying leverage through  technology  capital or media anyone can accumulate  wealth  with enough time and effort this type of  pithy tweet  is sometimes criticized as fortune  cookie twitter and when done  poorly tweets like these can come off as  cocky but naval has a great explanation  about why he tries to boil things down  so much  and this is where twitter is great for  me is i try to understand something and  then i try to write it down in such a  way  that i could remember it just the basic  hook that'll point towards the  deeper understanding and i'm forced to  explain it to people and that's how i  know i understand some things  the density of information exists on a  wide spectrum  on one end you might have a textbook or  academic paper  with copious sources and data supporting  each argument  and on the other end there are these  fortune cookie tweets but that doesn't  necessarily mean that one is better than  the other  you have to consider the goal naval is  taking his own advice here  and using leverage as effectively as  possible  he could probably spend months writing a  book that would sell a few thousand  copies  or he could write these uniquely terse  tweets that reach millions of people  and it makes sense for him to cast as  wide a net as possible  because he invests at the earliest stage  and wants lots of entrepreneurs to use  angellist  and naval isn't just focused on  entrepreneurs anymore he's also  innovating on the way angel investors  raise capital for their  funds in 2020 angellist announced the  rolling fund  to allow more people to get into angel  investing the rolling fund is unique in  a few ways  generally fund managers need to raise  their entire fund's capital  in a short period of time but rolling  funds are  always open meaning they can accept new  investments at any time  this also lets the fund capitalize on  big successes  to raise more money for the fund so if a  portfolio company breaks out  and raises a huge round of funding a  rolling fund could immediately add a  bunch of new limited partners  and then be able to make bigger  investments immediately  the second big innovation with rolling  funds involves marketing  typically managers cannot market their  funds publicly  which is why you'll never see an  advertisement to invest in a vc firm  but rolling funds fall under a special  sec  exemption called rule 506 c  marketing securities directly to the  public is always controversial  because there is a real risk that stock  promoters will take advantage of  unsophisticated investors  we've seen leonardo dicaprio play the  role of one of these penny stock  scammers in the wolf of wall street  and ben affleck played a similar role in  boiler room  both great films but the fact that  certain startup investments have  performed phenomenally well  over the past few decades and average  investors have not had access to that  asset class whatsoever  makes a really good case for allowing  unaccredited investors  to gain exposure to startup equity  markets  like i discussed in my earlier video on  chamath palihapatea and spax  financial innovation will always come  with risks and plenty of criticism  naval is undoubtedly aware of the fact  that scammers could try to abuse rolling  funds  and that could hurt the entire industry  i personally hope that these rolling  funds  get stress tested and wind up having a  positive impact on the startup ecosystem  as an entrepreneur i'm generally in  favor of anything that will bring more  funding dollars  into the early stage sector of the  economy since that will support  innovation and allow more people to  start companies but it's always  important to weigh the societal costs  and risks of abuse against these  financial innovations so we don't end up  with another 2007 financial crisis on  our hands  so it's 2021 now angel list is bigger  than ever and naval looks poised to  dramatically reshape startup financing  but what about all that epinions drama  between neval and benchmark capital  fortunately it looks like they were able  to reconcile somewhat  and let it be water under the bridge in  2016  bill gurley one of the vcs that neval  sued sat down with kara swisher on a  podcast and discussed the e pinion's  lawsuit  here's what gurley had to say about  noval ceo was naval robbie  yeah that's right and interesting story  about him  we ended up splitting uh from him and  there ended up being a lawsuit  and this was i don't know 16 17 years  ago and  and i had always thought of naval as one  of the the smartest people i'd ever met  but  when someone sues you you tend to get on  the other side of things and  just this past winter i was in um  at a ski event that chris sacca put  together and he was invited we sat down  and we talked for the first time  yeah it was awesome it was just like it  reminded me of why we worked together  in the first place but i regret that we  spent 16 years not chatting  he's obviously gone on to do uh do some  spectacular  it's great to see that they were able to  let bygones be bygones and move on from  the  opinions drama and in an interesting  twist both naval  and gurley wound up making a fortune  when they invested early in uber  naval was in the seed round and gurley  led the a round  so the big takeaway for me here is that  success in business  requires the right amount of aggression  if someone is really taking advantage of  you  a lawsuit might be your best bet but  there isn't much to be gained from  trashing them publicly  ultimately other opportunities will come  around and if you work hard enough you  will eventually find success  naval made the best out of a tough  situation and was seen as an  outcast in silicon valley for quite some  time but his willingness to fight for  founders  paid off as the valley shifted to a more  founder-friendly model  and his experience in the trenches  became something that founders loved  but what do you think of naval let me  know in the comments if you liked the  video and how it shaped your view of  silicon valley  and if you like this type of video  please consider subscribing to the  channel  i have a bunch more in the pipeline if  you want to get in touch with me  you can message me on twitter and as  always thanks for watching

